While I agree that spellcasters should be using spells more than
weapons, I'd have to disagree that the 'choice' of spells make the game
interesting. Once you get past the early game, you only use a handful
of spells, and they'll all be at 0% failure by the time you hit the
castle. The rest of your spellbooks you might as well chuck into a
C)oH. You use mainly magic missile, identify, light, magic mapping,
FoD, with maybe a few "special situation" spells in reserve, like jump,
force bolt, StF. Wizards have a very clear optimum set of spells, just
as they have a very clear optimum weapon and a pretty clear optimum
armour kit.

Personally I'd like to see spellcasters becoming more specialised
according to choices and events in-game: Perhaps at quest time wizards
could make a choice between various level advancement paths (you can
choose to advance towards necromancer, sorceror or white mage, all with
different spell strengths), or maybe spellcasting could be affected by
different armours (coloured armour affects spell classes) or props
(quarterstaffs, familiars, wands, artifacts), or maybe even advancement
in one spell skill could hinder advancement in another (get to expert
in attack and you find you can't get healing past basic). Something
alnog those lines to give wizards a little more character, as opposed
to the all-round unstoppable powerhouses of death that they inevitably
are by the time they get the Eye.



